b/ref 17592186045464 13194139534775 true] [17592186134198 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S = Span{a_{1}, a_{2}}$ where\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is orthogonal. Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139534775 false] [17592186134198 1100 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139534775 true] [17592186134198 1145 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-1)}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,9\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 11+(-1)\\cdot (-7)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{11}^{2}+(-7)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{171}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{57}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{57}}{9})≈{32.98}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139534775 true] [17592186134230 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534775 true] [17592186134230 1100 "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r = sv + tw$, where $s, t ∈ R$, and\n\n$v = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, w = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w$." 13194139534775 false] [17592186134230 1100 "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" 13194139534775 true] [17592186134230 1145 "Sinc